// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/trap_trampoline.vuma
// x86_64 trap entry/exit trampolines — TrapFrame layout + externs.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// This file is the x86_64-specific swap point for trap/IRQ handling. It
// declares the TrapFrame layout (the register-save area pushed by the
// trap_entry stub and popped by trap_exit) and the extern functions the
// trap dispatcher (K3b) and IRQ subsystem (K3c) call into. In hosted
// mode every extern resolves to `__ffi_fallback_stub` (returns 0, void
// calls no-op). In bare-metal mode (Wave K11) the same names link against
// REAL asm stubs in womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/trap.S.
//
// ── TrapFrame register-save order ───────────────────────────────────────────
// On an interrupt or exception, the x86_64 CPU pushes (in this order):
//     SS, RSP, RFLAGS, CS, RIP                  (5 words, CPU-pushed)
// Some exceptions also push an error code:
//     error_code                                (1 word, CPU-pushed for
//                                               #SS/#PF/#GP/#DF/#TS/#NP/
//                                               #SS/#AC/#MC; 0 otherwise)
// The trap stub (one per vector — generated by a macro in trap.S) pushes:
//     vector_number                             (1 word, stub-pushed)
// The trap_entry common path then pushes the GP registers in this order:
//     RAX, RBX, RCX, RDX, RSI, RDI, RBP,
//     R8, R9, R10, R11, R12, R13, R14, R15      (15 words, trap_entry)
//
// Total: 5 (CPU) + 1 (error) + 1 (vector) + 15 (GP) = 22 words = 176 bytes.
//
// IMPORTANT (contract-count discrepancy): the K3a contract's header
// comment says "24 registers × 8 bytes = 192 bytes" and its tail
// "IMPORTANT" note says "21 u64 fields = 168 bytes". NEITHER matches
// the layout it actually specifies — the layout block below has 22
// fields (count: 7 named GPs rax..rbp + 8 GPs r8..r15 + vector +
// error_code + rip + cs + rflags + rsp + ss = 22), giving 22 × 8 = 176
// bytes. The order of the fields MUST match trap.S exactly (K11 will
// write trap.S to match this layout), so this file keeps the 22-field
// layout verbatim from the contract and just corrects the byte count
// in the prose here. The trap dispatcher in K3b will index into this
// layout by field name (State<TrapFrame> accessors), NOT by raw
// offset, so the exact count matters for symbol layout but not for
// dispatcher correctness — every field is named.
//
// ── Extern "C" trampolines (IDT + IRQ + CR2) ────────────────────────────────
// Hosted: __ffi_fallback_stub (xor eax, eax; ret — returns 0, no-op for
//         void functions). The self-test's main() never calls any of these
//         (so the 0-return is not exercised from this file); they exist for
//         the K3b dispatcher / K3c IRQ subsystem / K3d syscall layer to
//         link against.
// Bare-metal (K11): real asm stubs registered in
//         src/codegen/src/x86_64/mod.rs::build_runtime_syscall_stubs:
//     idt_load(idt_ptr)
//         — `lidt [rdi]; ret` (RDI = pointer to a 10-byte IDT descriptor
//           struct: 16-bit limit + 64-bit base. Loads the IDT register,
//           arming all 256 interrupt vectors).
//     irq_mask(irq) / irq_unmask(irq)
//         — Program the 8259 PIC's mask register. K11 stub:
//           `mov al, 0xFF; out 0x21, al` (mask all) / `mov al, 0xFE;
//           out 0x21, al` (unmask IRQ 0 only — the timer). The `irq`
//           argument selects a finer-grained bit (K11 will refine the
//           stub to compute `mov al, ~(1 << irq); out 0x21, al`). In
//           hosted mode the stub is a no-op.
//     pic_eoi(irq)
//         — `mov al, 0x20; out 0x20, al; ret` (send End-Of-Interrupt to
//           the PIC's command port — required after every hardware IRQ
//           so the PIC will deliver the next one. Non-specific EOI; K11
//           may add a specific-EOI path for the cascaded slave PIC).
//     cr2_read() -> u64
//         — `mov rax, cr2; ret` (read the CR2 register — the page-fault
//           linear address. The trap dispatcher reads CR2 in the #PF
//           handler so it can pass the faulting vaddr to the page-fault
//           handler in K3b).
//
// ── Why re-declare nothing here? ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// VUMA has no `import` (Open Work §7), so every kernel module re-declares
// the layouts it uses byte-identically. THIS file declares TrapFrame for
// the FIRST time (no prior VUMA file has it); the K3b dispatcher, K3c IRQ
// subsystem, and K3d syscall layer will each re-declare this same layout
// from this file's contract. The byte-identical-redeclaration invariant
// (K2c "extern signatures MUST stay byte-identical") extends to layouts
// too — K3b's `dispatch_trap(tf: State<TrapFrame>)` MUST see the same
// field offsets, field names, and field types as this file.
//
// ── PMT discipline ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NO `*ptr`, `&x`, `allocate`, or `free` anywhere in this file. The
// TrapFrame is a 22-word struct held in a `___pmt_buffer`-backed
// State<TrapFrame>; the CALLER allocates it via `state_new(TrapFrame)`
// and passes it by reference to `trap_frame_init` / `trap_page_fault_addr`
// (init-style API — see "Why init-style?" in womb/kernel/mm/pmm.vuma for
// the codegen rationale). The externs' `idt_ptr: Address` parameter is a
// plain Address (SysV AMD64 RDI), NOT a State<T> — no #[borrow] needed
// because there is no State<T> to invalidate. None of the externs take a
// State<T> argument, so there is no use-after-invalidate risk from any
// of them.
//
// ── VUMA has no `import` ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// This file is self-contained. If a future dispatcher (K3b) wants to
// read `tf.vector`, it must re-declare the TrapFrame layout byte-identically
// to the one here (same pattern as womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/mm_trampoline.vuma
// re-declaring PageTable byte-identically to womb/kernel/mm/vmm.vuma).
//
// ── Build & verify (hosted x86_64) ──────────────────────────────────────────
//     ./target/release-fast/compile_dump \
//         womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/trap_trampoline.vuma \
//         /tmp/trap_trampoline.bin x86_64 --verify
//     /tmp/trap_trampoline.bin ; echo "exit=$?"
// Expected: IVE Pass, exit=0 (the 4 self-test checks all pass).

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Layouts.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The x86_64 TrapFrame. The field order MUST match trap.S exactly (K11
// will write trap.S to match). 22 fields × 8 bytes = 176 bytes. See the
// header comment for the push-order derivation (CPU-pushed + stub-pushed
// + trap_entry-pushed).
layout TrapFrame = {
    rax: u64,
    rbx: u64,
    rcx: u64,
    rdx: u64,
    rsi: u64,
    rdi: u64,
    rbp: u64,
    r8: u64,
    r9: u64,
    r10: u64,
    r11: u64,
    r12: u64,
    r13: u64,
    r14: u64,
    r15: u64,
    vector: u64,      // interrupt vector number (0..255)
    error_code: u64,  // CPU error code (0 if the exception pushes none)
    rip: u64,         // instruction pointer at the trap
    cs: u64,          // code segment
    rflags: u64,      // flags register
    rsp: u64,         // stack pointer
    ss: u64,          // stack segment
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Extern "C" trampolines — IDT load, PIC mask/unmask/EOI, CR2 read.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Hosted: __ffi_fallback_stub (returns 0, void calls no-op). K11 swaps
// these for real asm stubs registered in
// src/codegen/src/x86_64/mod.rs::build_runtime_syscall_stubs.
extern "C" {
    // Load the Interrupt Descriptor Table. `idt_ptr` is a SysV AMD64
    // RDI pointing at a 10-byte IDT descriptor struct (16-bit limit +
    // 64-bit base). K11 stub: `lidt [rdi]; ret`. Called once during
    // early boot to arm all 256 interrupt vectors.
    fn idt_load(idt_ptr: Address);
    // Mask an IRQ line at the 8259 PIC. `irq` is the IRQ number 0..15.
    // K11 stub: `mov al, 0xFF; out 0x21, al` (mask all — refined in K11
    // to per-IRQ bit-masking via `mov al, ~(1 << irq); out 0x21, al`).
    fn irq_mask(irq: u8);
    // Unmask an IRQ line at the PIC. K11 stub:
    // `mov al, 0xFE; out 0x21, al` (unmask IRQ 0 — refined in K11 to
    // per-IRQ bit-clearing). Pairs with irq_mask.
    fn irq_unmask(irq: u8);
    // Send End-Of-Interrupt to the PIC. Must be called at the end of
    // every hardware-IRQ handler so the PIC will deliver the next one.
    // K11 stub: `mov al, 0x20; out 0x20, al; ret` (non-specific EOI to
    // the master PIC's command port).
    fn pic_eoi(irq: u8);
    // Read the CR2 register — the page-fault linear address. K11 stub:
    // `mov rax, cr2; ret`. Used by trap_page_fault_addr below so the
    // trap dispatcher (K3b) can pass the faulting vaddr to the
    // page-fault handler.
    fn cr2_read() -> u64;
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Helpers.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Init-style API (caller allocates `tf` via state_new(TrapFrame), passes
// by reference). The State<TrapFrame> param here is a regular VUMA-fn
// borrow (NOT an extern #[borrow] — VUMA fn calls don't need the
// attribute, only extern "C" calls do, because the codegen tracks the
// State's lifetime through regular fn calls but conservatively
// invalidates on extern calls). See womb/kernel/mm/vmm.vuma::vaddr_parts
// for the same pattern (regular fn writing through a State<T> param).

// Initialize a TrapFrame to zeros. Useful both for the self-test and
// for the early-boot allocator (the trap dispatcher can call this on a
// scratch TrapFrame before populating it from the live save area, so
// stale stack garbage in the unused-error-code case can't leak into the
// handler). Writes all 22 fields to 0.
fn trap_frame_init(tf: State<TrapFrame>) {
    tf.rax = 0;
    tf.rbx = 0;
    tf.rcx = 0;
    tf.rdx = 0;
    tf.rsi = 0;
    tf.rdi = 0;
    tf.rbp = 0;
    tf.r8 = 0;
    tf.r9 = 0;
    tf.r10 = 0;
    tf.r11 = 0;
    tf.r12 = 0;
    tf.r13 = 0;
    tf.r14 = 0;
    tf.r15 = 0;
    tf.vector = 0;
    tf.error_code = 0;
    tf.rip = 0;
    tf.cs = 0;
    tf.rflags = 0;
    tf.rsp = 0;
    tf.ss = 0;
}

// Get the faulting address for a page fault (vector 14). Reads CR2 via
// the cr2_read extern. `tf` is passed for API symmetry with the other
// trap helpers (K3b's dispatch_trap will have a State<TrapFrame> in
// hand and call this without juggling locals); the function does not
// read any field of `tf` directly — the faulting address lives in the
// CR2 register, not in the saved frame.
//
// In hosted mode cr2_read resolves to __ffi_fallback_stub (returns 0),
// so this returns 0. K3b's page-fault handler will need to special-case
// the hosted path (hosted mode never delivers real page faults — the
// host kernel does); in bare-metal mode (K11) cr2_read returns the
// actual CR2 value.
fn trap_page_fault_addr(tf: State<TrapFrame>) -> u64 {
    return cr2_read();
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Self-test.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Allocate a TrapFrame via state_new, zero it via trap_frame_init, then
// verify:
//   (1) trap_frame_init cleared rax (representative GP register).
//   (2) trap_frame_init cleared vector.
//   (3) Writing 14 to vector (the page-fault vector) round-trips.
//   (4) Writing 4096 to rip round-trips — also confirms field-write +
//       field-read through State<TrapFrame> works for a non-zero value
//       larger than 255 (catches any accidental u8 truncation in the
//       codegen's State-access lowering).
//
// Per the K3a contract's "IMPORTANT" rule: all non-zero constants in
// the self-test use DECIMAL (4096, not 0x1000) — decimal avoids the
// parser's hex-literal path (parse_int_radix width-extension subtleties
// — see womb/kernel/arch/x86_64/pt.vuma header for the same rationale).
//
// Returns 0 on success, 1..4 on the corresponding failure (so a future
// CI failure pinpoints the broken check).

fn main() -> i32 {
    // Self-test: init a TrapFrame, verify fields, check vector.
    let tf = state_new(TrapFrame);
    trap_frame_init(tf);
    if tf.rax != 0 { return 1; }
    if tf.vector != 0 { return 2; }
    tf.vector = 14;  // page fault
    if tf.vector != 14 { return 3; }
    tf.rip = 4096;
    if tf.rip != 4096 { return 4; }
    return 0;
}
